\use{document}
\use{railroad}

\title{Railroad Diagrams from EBNF}
\author{the \texttt{railroad} package}
\maketitle

The \texttt{railroad} package turns a grammar written in W3C-style EBNF into a railroad
(syntax) diagram, one picture per rule. What better grammar to draw than the one the package
itself reads? Each rule below is rendered straight from its EBNF text by \texttt{\\railroad}.

\section{The shape of a grammar}

A grammar is one or more rules; each rule names an expression. An expression is a choice of
terms, a term is a row of factors, and a factor is an item with an optional repetition mark.

\railroad{
  grammar    ::= rule+
}

\railroad{
  rule       ::= name "::=" expression
}

\railroad{
  expression ::= term ("|" term)*
}

\railroad{
  term       ::= factor+
}

\railroad{
  factor     ::= primary ("?" | "*" | "+")?
}

\section{Items}

A primary is a name (a nonterminal), a terminal, or a parenthesised expression. A terminal
is a quoted literal or a character class.

\railroad{
  primary    ::= name | terminal | "(" expression ")"
}

\railroad{
  terminal   ::= '"' chars '"' | "'" chars "'" | charclass
}

\section{All at once}

A single \texttt{\\railroad} call may hold several rules; they are drawn in turn.

\railroad{
  digit  ::= [0-9]
  number ::= digit+ ("." digit+)?
  sign   ::= "+" | "-"
  signed ::= sign? number
}
